WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.050
The anterior epithelium, here, which will become a

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.160
part, here, which is called the cornea,

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.469
there, you see,

4
00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:13.742
l epithelium, which is therefore a mesenchymal

5
00:00:13.782 --> 00:00:14.132
tissue,

6
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.150
whereas the conjunctival tissue,

7
00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.980
we could say it is an epithelial,

8
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.520
thus a limiting tissue.

9
00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:33.171
Then we have what is called the anterior chamber,

10
00:00:33.211 --> 00:00:34.959
the pupillary orifice.

11
00:00:37.228 --> 00:00:40.614
You have three major tunics that surround the eye.

12
00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.660
So it's as if they were three socks,

13
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.010
one inside the other.

14
00:00:47.208 --> 00:00:49.786
The first sock is called the sclera.

15
00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:54.768
It is continuous with the cornea anteriorly.

16
00:00:56.315 --> 00:00:58.875
transparent, then you have the limbus,

17
00:00:58.915 --> 00:01:00.275
then the white part.

18
00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:01.895
This is the first layer.

19
00:01:01.935 --> 00:01:04.135
I remove this first sock, and then,

20
00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:07.858
we encounter an important layer called the choroid.

21
00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:12.071
And the choroid, we see here, you see,

22
00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:15.459
which is a layer that is extremely vascularized.

23
00:01:15.761 --> 00:01:18.621
And this choroid continues anteriorly,

24
00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:21.338
here, with what is called the iris.

25
00:01:24.926 --> 00:01:28.936
Iridology is a reading of the mesodermal vascular

26
00:01:28.976 --> 00:01:29.366
system.

27
00:01:31.492 --> 00:01:34.922
By observing the eye, you can see information about

28
00:01:34.962 --> 00:01:37.742
trophic changes of a vascular nature.

29
00:01:38.072 --> 00:01:41.292
This is expressed in the iris, which are sensors

30
00:01:41.332 --> 00:01:43.872
where they can store, for example,

31
00:01:43.912 --> 00:01:44.302
of sulfur.

32
00:01:44.332 --> 00:01:46.312
We can store a lot of things.

33
00:01:47.512 --> 00:01:48.862
And we can learn to read.

34
00:01:48.902 --> 00:01:51.482
There is a mapping of how it will be arranged.

35
00:01:52.282 --> 00:01:54.512
there will be another relationship that will be

36
00:01:54.552 --> 00:01:56.742
important, which is the ciliary processes and then

37
00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:59.432
there will be a relationship with what we call the

38
00:01:59.472 --> 00:01:59.971
suspensory

39
00:02:00.542 --> 00:02:03.342
muscles of the iris, which we will see in detail

40
00:02:03.382 --> 00:02:05.109
later to understand their physiology.

41
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.700
So we see the crystalline lens, the different

42
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.226
chambers that will appear, the pupillary orifice,

43
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:15.781
Here, a little further,

44
00:02:17.152 --> 00:02:19.910
in this choroid with the iris, you see.

45
00:02:20.353 --> 00:02:22.873
which will form an important link with what is

46
00:02:22.913 --> 00:02:25.042
called the suspensory ligament.

47
00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.996
There is also a ciliary muscle; there is an

48
00:02:30.036 --> 00:02:32.288
accommodation system that will occur.

49
00:02:33.541 --> 00:02:36.831
Well, this accommodation system involves this lens,

50
00:02:36.871 --> 00:02:38.921
which we call the crystalline lens,

51
00:02:38.961 --> 00:02:41.741
by the ciliary muscles, with the ora serrata;

52
00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:43.548
well, we'll see that later,

53
00:02:43.998 --> 00:02:47.168
which will increase, change the shape of my lens

54
00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:50.218
slightly, to allow the image to integrate correctly

55
00:02:50.258 --> 00:02:51.926
on the retinal plane.

56
00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.776
First layer, the sclera.

57
00:03:00.197 --> 00:03:02.509
Second layer, the choroid.

58
00:03:02.784 --> 00:03:04.861
Third layer, the retina.

59
00:03:06.744 --> 00:03:08.854
The nervous tissue, the deepest,

60
00:03:08.894 --> 00:03:11.434
which is truly the expression of the brain.

61
00:03:11.889 --> 00:03:15.598
With the colobomatous cleft, which gives rise to the

62
00:03:15.638 --> 00:03:15.988
anomaly.

63
00:03:16.438 --> 00:03:19.898
for the hyaloid artery and vessels which will form

64
00:03:19.938 --> 00:03:21.866
the retinal vessels, etc.

65
00:03:24.706 --> 00:03:27.745
Colobomatous cleft, do you understand what it was?

66
00:03:27.775 --> 00:03:30.055
With, you see, this remnant here,

67
00:03:30.095 --> 00:03:31.985
then this closure that will occur.

68
00:03:32.822 --> 00:03:36.621
So the retina, in fact, is the innermost layer of

69
00:03:36.661 --> 00:03:37.011
the eye.

70
00:03:37.798 --> 00:03:40.394
And so, where does this retina come from?

71
00:03:42.946 --> 00:03:45.286
Do you remember this first vesicle?

72
00:03:45.326 --> 00:03:48.536
this small sphere that emerges, which we call the

73
00:03:48.576 --> 00:03:50.407
primary optic vesicle,

74
00:03:50.705 --> 00:03:53.475
which will make a movement like this,

75
00:03:53.515 --> 00:03:55.661
you see, like a calyx, a cup.

76
00:03:57.703 --> 00:03:59.953
It is made of two layers, like this,

77
00:03:59.993 --> 00:04:02.553
you see, an inner layer, an outer layer.

78
00:04:02.885 --> 00:04:05.705
And this is where you understand what is called a

79
00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:06.725
retinal detachment.

80
00:04:06.765 --> 00:04:08.945
We can clearly see these two layers.

81
00:04:10.218 --> 00:04:12.788
Well, when people have a retinal detachment,

82
00:04:12.828 --> 00:04:15.638
it is this layer that expands, it is this space

83
00:04:15.678 --> 00:04:18.658
between the inner leaflet and the outer leaflet of

84
00:04:18.698 --> 00:04:19.118
the retina.

85
00:04:20.268 --> 00:04:22.768
There will be another very thin layer here,

86
00:04:22.808 --> 00:04:24.888
which will be very important later,

87
00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:27.429
it is what is called the pigmentary layer.

88
00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:32.214
You will discuss this, I think, in detail tomorrow,

89
00:04:32.254 --> 00:04:34.964
it is the entire layer of this retina with the

90
00:04:35.004 --> 00:04:37.434
pigmentary layer, and then what are called the

91
00:04:37.474 --> 00:04:40.562
photoreceptors, which are of the type that you...

92
00:04:40.707 --> 00:04:42.717
know as cones and rods.

93
00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:46.607
But you also have a layer called the ganglion cells.

94
00:04:46.647 --> 00:04:49.257
It is truly an important layer for

95
00:04:49.537 --> 00:04:52.307
information transfer to enable transduction.

96
00:04:52.347 --> 00:04:55.457
It is the conversion of a photon into electrical

97
00:04:55.497 --> 00:04:55.847
energy.

98
00:04:56.127 --> 00:04:58.273
Change members to sea.

